    text: {
      zh: "佛告阿難及韋提希：「眾寶國土，一一界上有五百億寶樓，其樓閣中有無量諸天，作天伎樂。又有樂器懸處虛空，如天寶幢不鼓自鳴。此眾音中，皆說念佛、念法、念比丘僧。此想成已，名為粗見極樂世界寶樹、寶地、寶池，是為總觀想，名第六觀。若見此者，除無量億劫極重惡業，命終之後必生彼國。作是觀者，名為正觀。若他觀者，名為邪觀。」",
      en: "The Buddha told Ānanda and Vaidehī: “On the jeweled land, on each boundary are fifty billion jewel pavilions; within the pavilions are immeasurable gods performing heavenly music. Also musical instruments hang in space like heavenly jewel banners, sounding without being struck. Within these sounds all speak of mindfulness of Buddha, Dharma, and bhikṣus. When this thought is accomplished, this is called a coarse seeing of the jewel trees, ground, and pools of the Land of Ultimate Bliss — this is the general contemplation, called the sixth contemplation. One who sees this removes immeasurable billions of kalpas of extremely heavy evil karma; after life ends one will surely be born in that land. One who does this contemplation is called correct contemplation; one who contemplates otherwise is called wrong contemplation.”"
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "佛陀告訴阿難與韋提希：在這片眾寶構成的國土中，每一處邊界之上都有五百億座寶樓，樓閣之中住著無量諸天，正在演奏天界的伎樂；還有樂器懸掛在虛空之中，如同天界的寶幢一般，不用敲擊便能自然發聲。這些聲音之中，全都在演說憶念佛、憶念法、憶念僧的道理。這個觀想成就之後，便算是粗略見到了極樂世界的寶樹、寶地、寶池——這就是總觀想，稱為第六觀。若能見到這樣的景象，便能消除無量億劫的極重惡業，命終之後必定往生彼國。修此觀者，稱為正觀；若以其他方式觀想，則稱為邪觀。這一觀之所以稱為「總觀想」，是因為它把前面日、水、地、樹、池五觀累積的細節暫時放下，退一步看整體格局——先是一一分項地精細觀想，再回到「粗見」的整體印象，兩種觀看方式交替使用，既不因貪求細節而迷失方向，也不因只看大局而流於空泛。經文說此觀能除「無量億劫極重惡業」，果報之重超過前五觀，也暗示能在細節與整體之間自由切換的觀照力，本身就是一種更深的定力。",
      en: "The Buddha tells Ānanda and Vaidehī: throughout this land of many jewels, every boundary bears fifty billion jeweled pavilions, within which immeasurable gods perform heavenly music, and musical instruments hang in the open sky like heavenly jeweled banners, sounding of themselves without being struck. Within these sounds is proclaimed the recollection of the Buddha, the Dharma, and the Sangha. Once this contemplation is accomplished, one has a coarse view of the jeweled trees, ground, and pools of the Land of Ultimate Bliss — this is the general contemplation, the sixth contemplation. Whoever sees this removes immeasurable billions of kalpas of the heaviest evil karma, and at life's end will surely be born in that land. To practice this contemplation is called correct contemplation; to contemplate otherwise is called wrong contemplation. This contemplation is called \"general\" because it sets aside, for a moment, the fine detail accumulated across the previous five contemplations of sun, water, ground, trees, and pools, stepping back to view the whole layout — alternating between fine, itemized visualization and a return to the coarse overall impression, so that the practitioner neither loses direction chasing detail nor drifts into vagueness by seeing only the big picture. That this contemplation removes \"immeasurable billions of kalpas of the heaviest evil karma,\" a result heavier than any of the first five, suggests that the very ability to move freely between detail and whole is itself a deeper form of concentration."
    },
